  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO-506695</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam    and others  have increasingly raised the alarm on growing global carbon inequality pointing out the unequal distribution of carbon emissions and their effects on the environment and society. Specifically, high-income individuals and nations tendto produce more carbon emissions per capita than low-income individuals and nations, while also being less vulnerable to the impacts of climate change.
Land-based carbon projects increasingly promoted as a means to achieve climate (net-zero) targets andas a source of climate finance in the Global South, present unique opportunities and risks for small-scale producers and communities. These projects, designed to generate credits often destined for the voluntary carbon market, may provide new sources of income but also pose substantial risks, such as land grabs and displacement.
Building on the analysis presented in Oxfam&apos;s 2021 Tightening the Net report, this research will assess the community benefits and risks of land-based carbon projects for small-scale producers. Oxfam America (OUS), inpartnership with Oxfam in Cambodia (OiC), will gather evidence on these projects&apos; social impacts, with particular attention given to benefits, risks, gender equity, and the rights of involved stakeholders. This evidence will inform our policy and advocacy effortsto protect community and human rights, land rights, and gender equity.</narrative>
  </description>
